> Long-time user of unimpaired, with bracketed I miss [unimpaired-toggling ](https://github.com/tpope/vim-unimpaired/blob/master/doc/unimpaired.txt#L77-L93).

@leiserfg, yes, option toggling is a vital part of workflow once you get used to it. As said above, this is a part of ['mini.basics'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/readmes/mini-basics.md) under [`mappings.option_toggle_prefix`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/doc/mini-basics.txt#L238) option. By default it creates mappings like `\s` for spell, etc., but prefix can be changed to be `yo` to match 'vim-unimpaired'.

To be honest, those kind of mappings are quite easy to replicate and customize manually in own config. [Here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/91f7a680fe5765b68456ab6e7b910d478da083b9/lua/mini/basics.lua#L584-L603) is all the code from 'mini.basics'.

OK, just to clarify, we often have combination of comment types in JS/TS files:

```js
// comment of line
const THING = 0;

/*
* JS Doc style comment
*/
const OTHER_THING = 1;
```

Was just hoping I could cycle through all of them with `]c`/`[c`.

> Which targets need `Enter` to confirm in your case?

I realised now that it's not connected to using other characters, but it happens when using `[` or `]` as well. Sorry for the confusion, I'm still new to the concepts of bracketed. 

This is the printout:
<img width="907" alt="Screenshot 2023-03-05 at 13 44 49" src="https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/7226476/222961194-5d03d68c-3471-41a5-803c-c539ddd9fd76.png">

In the file below, if my cursor is at line 8, at the `r` in the start of the word `require`. 

```lua
return {
  "echasnovski/mini.nvim",
  event = "VeryLazy",
  dependencies = { "JoosepAlviste/nvim-ts-context-commentstring", lazy = true },
  config = function()
    require("mini.pairs").setup()
    require("mini.statusline").setup()
    require("mini.comment").setup({
      hooks = {
        pre = function()
          require("ts_context_commentstring.internal").update_commentstring({})
        end,
      },
    })
    require("mini.bufremove").setup()
    require("mini.tabline").setup()
    require("mini.bracketed").setup()
  end,
}
```

@barrett-ruth 

```
        require("mini.bracketed").setup({
            oldfile = { suffix = "" },
        })
```

works for me.
